\pard\qc\b\fs36 TaskFlow Microservice Application\par
\fs30 Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment Practical\par
\b0\fs22\par
\pard\ql
\b Student:\b0 ______________________________\par
\b Date:\b0 ______________________________\par
\par
\b 1. Aim\b0\par
Implement a CI/CD pipeline using GitHub, GitHub Actions, Docker, Docker Hub, and Kubernetes for the TaskFlow cloud-native application.\par
\par
\b 2. CI/CD Concept\b0\par
Continuous Integration automatically validates code whenever it is pushed or a pull request is opened. Continuous Deployment builds and publishes container images, then deploys a selected version to Kubernetes.\par
\par
\b 3. Pipeline Flow\b0\par
\f1 GitHub push -> validation -> Docker image build -> Docker Hub push -> Kubernetes deployment -> health checks\par
\f0\par
\b 4. Required GitHub Secrets\b0\par
The GitHub repository must contain these Actions secrets:\par
\f1 DOCKERHUB_USERNAME\par
DOCKERHUB_TOKEN\par
\f0\par
Never place the Docker Hub token in source code or workflow output.\par
\par
\b 5. Workflow File\b0\par
The workflow is stored at:\par
\f1 .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml\par
\f0\par
It contains three jobs:\par
\tab validate\par
\tab publish-images\par
\tab deploy\par
\par
\b 6. Validate Job\b0\par
The validate job runs on GitHub-hosted Ubuntu and checks all four application packages:\par
\f1 frontend\par
services/user-service\par
services/task-service\par
services/stats-service\par
\f0\par
It installs dependencies with:\par
\f1 npm ci --ignore-scripts\par
\f0\par
The frontend runs:\par
\f1 npm run build\par
\f0\par
Backend test commands are executed when test scripts are available:\par
\f1 npm test --if-present\par
\f0\par
\b 7. Publish Images Job\b0\par
The publish job runs after validation succeeds. It logs in to Docker Hub using GitHub Secrets and builds four images:\par
\f1 <username>/taskflow-frontend\par
<username>/taskflow-user-service\par
<username>/taskflow-task-service\par
<username>/taskflow-stats-service\par
\f0\par
Each image receives two tags:\par
\f1 <commit-sha>\par
latest\par
\f0\par
The commit tag provides an immutable release version. The latest tag identifies the most recent main-branch build.\par
\par
\b 8. Kubernetes Deploy Job\b0\par
The deployment job is manual and uses a \f1 self-hosted\f0 GitHub Actions runner. A GitHub-hosted runner cannot directly access a local Docker Desktop Kubernetes cluster.\par
\par
The self-hosted runner must have:\par
\tab Docker\par
\tab kubectl\par
\tab Docker Desktop Kubernetes enabled\par
\tab Access to the taskflow namespace\par
\par
The deployment job performs:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/\par
kubectl set image deployment/frontend ...\par
kubectl set image deployment/user-service ...\par
kubectl set image deployment/task-service ...\par
kubectl set image deployment/stats-service ...\par
kubectl rollout status ...\par
kubectl exec ... /health\par
\f0\par
\b 9. Publish the Workflow\b0\par
From the project root:\par
\f1 git add .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml\par
git add TaskFlow_CICD_Practical.rtf\par
git commit -m "Add CI/CD pipeline"\par
git push origin main\par
\f0\par
\b 10. Run CI/CD\b0\par
Open the GitHub repository:\par
\f1 Actions -> TaskFlow CI/CD\par
\f0\par
For a normal push, the validate and publish jobs run automatically.\par
\par
For a manual run:\par
\tab Select \f1 Run workflow\f0 .\par
\tab Select the \f1 main\f0 branch.\par
\tab Set \f1 deploy\f0 to \f1 true\f0 only when a self-hosted runner is configured.\par
\tab Start the workflow.\par
\par
Take Screenshot 1: CI/CD workflow run.\par
\par
